=== Play <disambiguation>

==== Disambiguation

Play has several meanings. The one I want to focus on is transformative play,
the play of the metagame -- play which modifies the very rules of play. This
sort of play is entangled, in Noë's sense of the term. In addition to
transformative play, there is mechanical play (play broadly construed to include
e.g. physical notions of play), being-playful (play construed as a willingness
to mold or shift one's perspective, to _break_ the rules or to bend them), play
as rule-following (e.g. striving play, wherein one takes on the temporary goals
of a game so as to accomplish some meta-level goal).

Play as rule-following, our typical playful activity, is an interesting case
because it is simultaneously mechanical and meta-level. Our concerns are only
temporarily vested in the act of the game -- our larger goals live at a higher
level. Our goals in playing the game change the way in which the game is played.
For example if we are teaching a young person to play a game, we want to make
sure they maintain an interest by going easy on them, allowing them to win from
time to time. But if we are playing the game competitively, our brains kick into
high gear: what's wrapped up in the game is far more than an education, but
pride and identity, perhaps even prize money, a championship, one's livelihood.
So-called serious games also take on a more responsible role in our lives, for
example teaching nurses to cope with their jobs, or boot camp simulations
training soldiers for the battlefield. The history of games as a medium is tied
up with the development of war games in general, perhaps the most serious games
of them all.

How to consider ludic activity, i.e., messing around? Teasing, tussling, tossing
a ball around. As a kind of manipulative play, wherein the rules and boundaries
of our external world, be they social or physical, are tested?

-- Much of my analysis of play has to do with the _function_ of play more than
the phenomenology of it. There is not much discussion here of joy or desire.
That feels a bit out of scope -- I do not consider e.g. the evolutionary
psychology of play or its place in culture in large, but rather focus on its
role in our relationship to the Real (the unconscious, the noumenal, etc.), i.e.
its ontological and epistemological significance.

==== Taxonomy
Play happens when free movement occurs within a rigid structure. Let's break
that down. In what domains can play occur? What sort of structures give rise to
play? What is 'free movement?'

These terms are overly broad. But perhaps their breadth is an asset. Free
movement evokes the graceful swoops of a flycatcher, the stochastic movement of
thermodynamic particles, and the fancy of an active imagination in equal
measure. But these are vastly distinct domains: animal behavior, the movement of
atoms, and theoretical postulation. In each case this behavior is restricted by
bounds: the bird must fight against gravity and wind; the particles must respect
the forces acting upon and against them; and the mind is bound by the rules of
logic and conceptual framework in which it is situated. Without the wind and the
pull of the earth there is no flight; without a wall to bounce against there is
no heat pressure; without a constitutive framework there is no room for
theorization, no concepts to bounce back against or deductive rules to follow.
So we have established that *free movement cannot occur without restriction.*
The phrase 'free movement within a rigid structure' is in fact redundant: there
is no free movement without a rigid structure. But can we show the reverse? Does
a rigid structure presuppose the possibility of free movement?

This one is more difficult. It seems quite clear that a universe solely
consisting of static matter is possible, if not in fact more likely. But in this
case, the _static_ nature gives away the game: if there is nothing for it to
bound, is it a structure at all? Even the empty set provides a base structure:
the _possibility_ to be filled. A monistic universe, consisting of only a single
fact, perhaps of _nothing at all,_ is still structured by the rules of logical
necessity, still conceptually bound by its negation. So it is the structure of
our discourse that is limiting us. To solve this we need to take a step back and
look at the structure which comprises our conceptual schemata.

Here I take inspiration from Zen Buddhist accounts of the structure of
consciousness. From whence do our conceptions arise in the first place? Nishida
calls this the place of absolute nothingness (_basho no zettai mu_). De Sassure
and Derrida might consider it the space in which the differential between
concepts occurs, the background on which _différance_ is made manifest. For
Heidegger it is similar to _das Nichts_ or _der Lichtung,_ the formless
nothingness from which all Being springs forth.

Each of these accounts point toward a meta-level consciousness, something that
is beyond the direct layer of perception. I take Albahari's term _witness
consciousness_ to describe this higher-level perception.

+ Structural rigidty
  - Mechanical play vs transformative play -- mechanical play occurs _within_
    the structure, is rule-bound; transformative play _changes_ the structure,
    occurs at a meta level
+ Domain
  - physical, mechanical, rule-bound
    - physical laws, rule-bound play, _deterministically automatable,_ "games"
      in the "game theory" sense.
    - This sense of play is not necessarily agentic. Agents definitely play like
      this, but not _only_ agents exhibit this sort of behavior.
      - Is this a credible notion of "play?" Is this not just 'stochastic
        behavior?' -- There seems to be a sort of _stategic incentive_ to reach
        an optimistic state. Physical phenomena certainly achieve this. Though
        it stretches the notion of play far beyond traditional credibility.
  - biological, chemical, transformative
    - agentic, theoretic, onto-epistemological -> a kind of transformative play
+ Levels
  - rule-bound play
  - metagaming
  - design
